Stream chunks are no longer GPU-cacheable. NullMoth's own change (not an upstream Mesa commit).
NVK appends each small queue push (context setup, heap and shader local memory binds) and each upload of the upload
queue to the current 64 KB stream chunk, right behind the previous one with 4-byte alignment, so a new push often
starts in a cache line the GPU has already read. Every release up to 2.9 mapped stream chunks GPU-cacheable
(NV01_MEMORY_SYSTEM with ATTR2_GPU_CACHEABLE_YES, a non-volatile PTE); the GPU's L2 does not snoop CPU writes and
coherence comes only from the L2 invalidate in the tail of each submission. The host fetches pushbuffer words ahead of
the engine, so it can read the next push before the previous submission's tail has run, and then sees that shared line
as it was before the CPU wrote the new words, and an invalid pushbuffer entry stops the channel. Stream chunks are now
plain system memory (volatile PTE), as the context's own pushbuffer, GPFIFO, semaphore and notifier pages already are.
Each stream line is read once, so nothing is lost by not caching it. The command pool's chunks are not changed and still
follow NVK_CMDPOOL_GPU_UNCACHED.

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_mem_stream.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_mem_stream.c
